  "text": "arsimp to solve this problem, we will need to use some concepts related to geometry and trigonometry. Here are the steps we will follow:\n\n1. Identify the given information.\n2. Set up a right triangle using the given information.\n3. Apply the Pythagorean theorem to find the distance between the center of the sphere and the point where the wire touches the plane.\n4. Use trigonometry to find the height of the cylinder.\n5. Apply the Pythagorean theorem again to find the radius of the cylinder's base.\n\nLet's start with step 1. We are given the following information:\n\n- The angle made by the wire with the plane is 60 degrees.\n- The sphere is 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane.\n- The radius of the sphere is 2 cm.\n\nNow, let's proceed to step 2. We will set up a right triangle using the given information. The wire is the hypotenuse, and we can denote the distance between the center of the sphere and the point where the wire touches the plane as \"a\". We can use trigonometry to find the height of the cylinder.\n\nTo find the distance \"a\", we will use the angle 60 degrees and the radius of the sphere, which is 2 cm. We will apply the sine function since we are given the hypotenuse and need to find the opposite side in the triangle:\n\n$\\sin(60\\degree) = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n$\\sin(60\\degree) = \\frac{2}{\\tan(60\\degree)a}$\n\n$\\tan(60\\degree)a = \\frac{2}{\\sin(60\\degree)}$\n\n$a = \\frac{2}{\\sin(60\\degree)} \\times \\tan(60\\degree)$\n\n$a = \\frac{2}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\times \\tan(60\\degree)$\n\n$a = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\sqrt{3}$\n\n$a = 4$\n\nNow, we have found the distance \"a\" to be 4 cm.\n\nTo find the height of the cylinder, we need to subtract the radius of the sphere from \"a\". This yields:\n\n$\\text{Height of the cylinder} = a - \\text{radius of the sphere}$\n\n$\\text{Height of the cylinder} = 4 - 2$\n\n$\\text{Height of the cylinder} = 2$\n\nNow, we have found the height of the cylinder to be 2 cm.\n\nFinally, we will proceed to step 5. We will use trigonometry again to find the radius of the cylinder's base. We will use the tangent function since we are given the adjacent side and need to find the opposite side in the triangle:\n\n$\\tan(60\\degree) = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}$\n\n$\\tan(60\\degree) = \\frac{y}{2}$\n\n$y = 2 \\times \\tan(60\\degree)$\n\n$y = 2 \\times \\sqrt{3}$\n\n$y = 2\\sqrt{3}$\n\nNow, we have found the radius of the cylinder's base to be $2\\sqrt{3}$ cm.\n\nTo summarize the steps we took, we identified the given information, set up a right triangle using the given information, applied trigonometry, found the distance between the center of the sphere and the point where the wire touches the plane, found the height of the cylinder, and applied trigonometry again to find the radius of the cylinder's base. Therefore, the radius of the cylinder's base is $2\\sqrt{3}$ cm. \\boxed{2\\sqrt{3}}",
